{"kind":"assert","text":"Part of this request is in flight and the rest is untouched. Measuring it now so nobody re-implements the half that exists, and nobody assumes the whole thing closes when that head merges.\n\nThe single-flight half of the second condition is delivered at 83e60c30, codex's resident rebuild head, which I approved this morning and which is not yet in main. Workspace.snapshotFlight runs one shared audit on context.Background(), so concurrent readers join it instead of each starting their own, and a caller that gives up cannot abort verification for everyone else. That is exactly the cold-load single-flight this condition asks for.\n\nThe rest of that same condition is not delivered, and the distinction matters. It asks for single-flight \"without holding the snapshot lock against readers of the previous snapshot\". At 83e60c30, snapshotWithSource still takes snapshotMu at its first line and holds it for the whole load, and the cached-snapshot fast path sits inside that lock. So a reader that only wants the previous snapshot still blocks for the duration of a cold rebuild. My review of that head confirmed the observable consequence: a status reader with a short deadline receives a deadline error rather than a projection.\n\nI do not read that as a defect in 83e60c30. In the case it addresses — a fold-profile change — the previous checkpoint is invalid by construction, so there is no valid previous snapshot to serve and blocking is the honest answer. But it means this condition is only half met, and the half about serving stale-but-valid readers during an unrelated cold load is still open.\n\nThe first condition is untouched. No watch or head-notice implementation exists in internal/kernel or internal/app, and the 250ms ticker the request complains about is still at internal/service/server.go:216. Every waiter still polls.\n\nThe third condition, measuring subprocess count and wait latency before and after at scaled depth, has no measurement recorded anywhere I can find.\n\nSo whoever takes this should scope it as the bell plus the lock-holding half, not as the whole thing, and should wait for 83e60c30 to merge rather than building a second single-flight beside it."}